--TEST--
an in-process response carries the status the program declared, and `200` where it declared none, asserted on both sides in one run — ADR 0079 § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bound named on both sides: the last value the program chose and the first
// one it did not. A `Core\Test\Response` that only ever reported what was
// declared would answer `0` for the program that declared nothing, and one
// that only ever reported `200` would lose the `201` -- either half alone
// prints plausibly, so both are asked in the same run.
//
// Two requests through the same program, told apart by the path they arrived
// with, which is also what pins that the *target* crosses and not only the
// match: the second path is claimed by no route, so the child is reading
// `Core\Request::path()` rather than a route row.
//
// The `catch` is the child, as in the two sibling cases.
try {
    var $declared = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/declares");
    echo "declared ", $declared->status(), " ", $declared->body(), "\n";
    var $silent = Core\Test::request(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/silent");
    echo "silent ", $silent->status(), " ", $silent->body(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    if (Core\Str::endsWith(Core\Request::path(), "/declares")) {
        Core\Response::setStatus(201);
        echo "made";
    } else {
        echo "said nothing";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
declared 201 made
silent 200 said nothing
